  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "A body of mass $9\\,\\text{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({e}^{2t})$ meters per second.  Find an expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "By Newton\u2019s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[3\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({e}^{2t})] \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({e}^{2t})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({e}^{2t})\\cdot 2{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =-6{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({e}^{2t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =9\\cdot [-6{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({e}^{2t})] \\\\  & =-54{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({e}^{2t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
